(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["physiology-exam-2-circulatory-physiology-2-quiz-version-2"]={"title":"Circulatory Physiology II — Physiology Exam 2 — Set 2","category":"Physiology","exam":"Exam 2","sem":"summer-1-2026","questions":[{"q":"What is the approximate normal resting coronary blood flow, and what fraction of total cardiac output does it represent?","choices":["About 2000 mL/min, roughly 50% of cardiac output","About 10 mL/min, a negligible fraction of cardiac output","About 225 mL/min, roughly 4-5% of total cardiac output","Coronary flow is not measured as a fraction of cardiac output"],"answer":2,"exp":"Normal resting coronary blood flow averages about 70 mL/min/100g of heart weight, or about 225 mL/min total, representing roughly 4-5% of total cardiac output. — Circulatory Physiology II.pptx, Slide 39"},{"q":"During which phase of the cardiac cycle does coronary blood flow decrease, and why?","choices":["During systole, because cardiac muscle contraction obstructs (compresses) the coronary vessels; the heart is mainly perfused during diastole","During diastole, because ventricular relaxation reduces aortic pressure and thus coronary perfusion pressure","During diastole, because closure of the aortic valve temporarily blocks blood flow into the coronary ostia at that time","Coronary flow is constant throughout the cardiac cycle, since the myocardium never compresses its own vessels"],"answer":0,"exp":"Coronary blood flow decreases during systole because cardiac muscle contraction compresses and obstructs the coronary vessels running through the myocardium; the heart muscle is therefore mainly perfused during diastole. — Circulatory Physiology II.pptx, Slide 40"},{"q":"What are the primary regulators of coronary blood flow, and how tightly is flow linked to myocardial oxygen need?","choices":["Only circulating catecholamines and angiotensin II, with coronary flow being inversely proportional to systemic blood pressure","Coronary flow is fixed by vessel diameter alone and is unrelated to myocardial oxygen consumption or metabolic byproducts","Local metabolic factors (O2 being major, plus adenosine, CO2, lactic acid, K+, NO); coronary flow is directly proportional to the myocardium's oxygen need/consumption","Only neural sympathetic control acting through the beta-1 adrenergic receptors, with coronary flow varying inversely with the heart rate"],"answer":2,"exp":"Coronary blood flow is primarily regulated by local metabolic factors — oxygen being the major one, along with adenosine, CO2, lactic acid, potassium, and nitric oxide (usually triggered by O2 changes) — with flow directly proportional to the cardiac muscle's oxygen need/consumption. — Circulatory Physiology II.pptx, Slide 41"},{"q":"Approximately what percentage of oxygen delivered in the coronary circulation is extracted at rest, and what does this imply about increasing oxygen delivery?","choices":["About 25% is extracted, leaving abundant reserve capacity to raise oxygen delivery through extraction alone","Nearly 100% is extracted, so any rise in demand can only be met by dramatically slowing heart rate","About 70% is extracted, leaving little room to increase oxygen availability other than by increasing coronary blood flow","Only about 40% is extracted, similar to skeletal muscle, leaving substantial room to raise extraction"],"answer":2,"exp":"About 70% of the oxygen in the coronary circulation is extracted at rest, leaving little to no room to increase oxygen availability by extracting more — meaning increased myocardial oxygen demand must be met primarily by increasing coronary blood flow. — Circulatory Physiology II.pptx, Slide 41"},{"q":"How does low oxygen in cardiomyocytes lead to increased coronary blood flow via adenosine?","choices":["Low oxygen decreases ATP degradation, reducing AMP and adenosine formation; the resulting drop in adenosine causes coronary vasoconstriction, further limiting flow to the ischemic tissue","Low oxygen increases ATP degradation to AMP, some of which is further degraded to release adenosine into cardiac tissue fluid, causing vasodilation; adenosine is later reabsorbed for ATP resynthesis","High oxygen increases ATP synthesis from AMP, releasing adenosine into the cardiac tissue fluid, which then causes coronary vasoconstriction rather than dilation","Low oxygen actually decreases the degradation of ATP to AMP, reducing local adenosine release and causing coronary vessels to mildly constrict rather than dilate"],"answer":1,"exp":"Low oxygen in cardiomyocytes increases cellular degradation of ATP to AMP; small portions of this are further degraded to release adenosine into the heart's tissue fluid, causing local coronary vasodilation — after which much of the adenosine is reabsorbed into cardiac cells to be reused for ATP production. — Circulatory Physiology II.pptx, Slide 42"},{"q":"How do epicardial and intramuscular coronary vessels differ in their adrenergic receptor populations, and what is the usual net effect of sympathetic stimulation?","choices":["Coronary vessels express only beta-2 adrenergic receptors throughout their length, with alpha receptors confined entirely to peripheral skeletal muscle vasculature","Epicardial vessels have more beta receptors while intramuscular arteries have more alpha receptors; sympathetic stimulation of the coronary vasculature usually produces net dilation","Epicardial coronary vessels have a preponderance of alpha receptors, while intramuscular arteries have more beta receptors; sympathetic stimulation usually causes slight overall coronary constriction","Both epicardial and intramuscular coronary vessels have identical, evenly mixed receptor populations, so sympathetic stimulation always produces uniform dilation"],"answer":2,"exp":"Epicardial coronary vessels have a preponderance of alpha receptors while intramuscular arteries have more beta receptors; sympathetic stimulation of coronary vessels can theoretically cause slight dilation or constriction but usually causes slight overall constriction, with some individuals showing disproportionately severe alpha-mediated vasospasm (vasospastic myocardial ischemia/angina) during excess sympathetic drive. — Circulatory Physiology II.pptx, Slide 43"},{"q":"What percentage of the heart's energy is normally derived from fatty acids at rest, and what happens metabolically during ischemia?","choices":["Ischemia dramatically increases fatty acid oxidation exclusively, sparing the glucose reserves entirely and producing no lactic acid buildup at all","The heart relies almost entirely on circulating ketone bodies for energy at rest, switching to fatty acids only during ischemic stress","About 100% of resting cardiac energy comes from glucose oxidation; ischemia then causes a shift toward exclusive fatty acid metabolism","About 70% from fatty acids at rest; ischemia forces reliance on anaerobic glycolysis, consuming glucose and producing lactic acid (a likely cause of cardiac pain)"],"answer":3,"exp":"Under resting conditions, cardiac muscle derives about 70% of its energy from fatty acids rather than carbohydrates; under anaerobic/ischemic conditions, the heart must rely on anaerobic glycolysis, consuming large quantities of glucose and producing lactic acid — likely a cause of cardiac pain during ischemia. — Circulatory Physiology II.pptx, Slide 44"},{"q":"What determines the degree of damage from coronary occlusion, and how quickly can collateral flow develop after a sudden occlusion?","choices":["Collateral circulation matters only in slowly progressive atherosclerosis; sudden occlusions always cause complete and irreversible infarction regardless of any anastomoses","Collateral vessels typically take several years to develop meaningful flow, so acute coronary occlusions cause maximal, unmodifiable damage regardless of anastomoses","Damage is determined largely by the degree of collateral circulation; small anastomoses dilate within seconds of sudden occlusion, and collateral flow can reach near-normal levels within about a month","Damage is determined solely by the diameter of the vessel that occludes; collateral anastomoses essentially do not exist within the coronary circulation"],"answer":2,"exp":"The degree of heart muscle damage from coronary occlusion is determined largely by the degree of collateral circulation; small anastomoses dilate within seconds of a sudden occlusion (though initially providing less than half the needed flow), and collateral flow often increases to near-normal levels within about a month, aiding recovery. — Circulatory Physiology II.pptx, Slide 45"},{"q":"What causes the first heart sound, and how does its pitch/loudness compare to the second heart sound?","choices":["A-V valve closure plus backflow of blood against the ventricular wall; it is louder and lower-pitched than the second heart sound","Reverberation of blood within the ventricles during rapid filling; it is softer and higher-pitched than the second heart sound","Semilunar valve closure alone, with no contribution from blood backflow; it is identical in pitch to the second heart sound","Atrial contraction against closed A-V valves; it is typically inaudible without amplification equipment"],"answer":0,"exp":"The first heart sound (S1) is caused by A-V valve closure plus backflow of blood against the ventricular wall; it is louder and lower-pitched than the second heart sound (S2), which is caused by aortic/pulmonic valve closure and is shorter and higher-pitched. — Circulatory Physiology II.pptx, Slide 47"},{"q":"What causes the third and fourth heart sounds, and when are they considered normal versus pathological?","choices":["Both sounds are produced exclusively by semilunar valve closure occurring earlier than normal, and are always considered entirely normal findings at any age, including in elderly hypertensive patients","S3 is generated by mitral valve closure and S4 is generated by tricuspid valve closure, with both occurring simultaneously alongside the first heart sound in every healthy adult","S3 and S4 are both caused by turbulent flow through stenotic semilunar valves during systole, exactly like the mechanism that produces the first and second heart sounds in every patient","S3 is caused by reverberation of blood in the ventricles (middle third of diastole); S4 is caused by atrial contraction late in diastole — both may be physiological in young children/thin people but are otherwise usually pathological"],"answer":3,"exp":"The third heart sound (S3) is caused by reverberation of blood in the ventricles during the middle third of diastole; the fourth heart sound (S4) is caused by atrial contraction (\"atrial kick\") late in diastole. Both are low pitched and can be physiologically audible in young children or thin people, but are otherwise usually pathological findings. — Circulatory Physiology II.pptx, Slide 48"},{"q":"What is the general function of the arterial baroreceptor reflex, and where are the key baroreceptors located?","choices":["Baroreceptors are located exclusively in the pulmonary veins and atrial walls, unrelated to systemic pressure","It is important only for regulating respiratory rate, with stretch receptors located in the bronchial walls","It is important for long-term pressure regulation only; baroreceptors are chemosensitive cells located in the renal afferent arterioles","It is important for short-term regulation of arterial pressure; baroreceptors (stretch receptors) are located in the carotid sinus and aortic arch"],"answer":3,"exp":"The arterial baroreceptor reflex is important in the short-term regulation of arterial pressure; it is initiated by stretch receptors (baroreceptors/pressoreceptors) located in the walls of the carotid sinus (at the carotid bifurcation) and the aortic arch. — Circulatory Physiology II.pptx, Slide 50"},{"q":"By what nerves do signals from the carotid sinus and aortic arch baroreceptors reach the nucleus tractus solitarius (NTS)?","choices":["Both the carotid sinus and aortic arch signals travel exclusively via the accessory nerve to the medulla","Both pathways terminate in the cerebellum instead of reaching the nucleus tractus solitarius","Carotid sinus signals travel via Hering's nerve to the glossopharyngeal nerve; aortic arch signals travel via the vagus nerve","Both carotid sinus and aortic arch signals travel exclusively through the hypoglossal nerve"],"answer":2,"exp":"Signals from the carotid sinus baroreceptors travel via Hering's nerve to the glossopharyngeal nerve (CN IX) and then to the nucleus tractus solitarius (NTS) of the medulla; signals from the aortic arch travel through the vagus nerve (CN X) to the NTS. — Circulatory Physiology II.pptx, Slide 51"},{"q":"Over what pressure range do carotid sinus baroreceptors respond, and at what pressure is the reflex most sensitive?","choices":["Baroreceptors respond equally at all pressures with no peak sensitivity","200-300 mm Hg, most sensitive at 250 mm Hg","60-180 mm Hg, most sensitive around 100 mm Hg","0-50 mm Hg, most sensitive at 25 mm Hg"],"answer":2,"exp":"Carotid sinus baroreceptors respond to pressures between about 60 and 180 mm Hg, with the reflex being most sensitive around a pressure of 100 mm Hg — near the normal mean arterial pressure. — Circulatory Physiology II.pptx, Slide 52"},{"q":"As arterial pressure rises and carotid sinus impulses increase, what two effects does this produce?","choices":["Only activation of the sympathetic vasoconstrictor system and adrenal catecholamine release, with no change in vagal tone","A transient increase in both vasoconstrictor and vagal activity that cancels out, leaving pressure unchanged","Activation of the vasoconstrictor center and inhibition of the vagal center — both working to raise pressure further","Inhibition of the vasoconstrictor center and activation of the vagal center — both working to lower pressure back toward normal"],"answer":3,"exp":"As arterial pressure increases and carotid sinus baroreceptor impulses increase, this causes (1) inhibition of the vasoconstrictor center and (2) activation of the vagal center — both actions work together to lower heart rate and vascular resistance, returning pressure toward normal. — Circulatory Physiology II.pptx, Slide 52"},{"q":"What happens to baroreceptor sensitivity when they are exposed to a sustained change in blood pressure?","choices":["Baroreceptor firing rate increases progressively over days of sustained hypertension, amplifying their long-term corrective effect","Baroreceptors \"reset\" and attenuate their potency in response to sustained pressure changes, which is why they are considered unimportant for long-term pressure control","Baroreceptors maintain a constant sensitivity indefinitely, which is why they dominate both the short-term and the long-term pressure regulation","Resetting occurs only with sudden pressure spikes and reverses completely within minutes, restoring full baseline sensitivity"],"answer":1,"exp":"Baroreceptors \"reset\" when exposed to sustained increases or decreases in blood pressure, attenuating their potency over time — this adaptation is why baroreceptors are considered unimportant for long-term arterial pressure control, even though they effectively buffer short-term/daily variations. — Circulatory Physiology II.pptx, Slide 54"},{"q":"What stimulates the carotid and aortic chemoreceptors, and at what pressure threshold do they typically become activated?","choices":["They are stimulated by rising blood glucose levels and are activated continuously regardless of the arterial pressure","They respond only to changes in plasma osmolarity and are located exclusively within the hypothalamus","They are stimulated by oxygen excess and CO2 depletion, and are activated only above 150 mm Hg","They are stimulated by oxygen lack, CO2 excess, or H+ excess, and are typically not stimulated until pressure falls below 80 mm Hg"],"answer":3,"exp":"Carotid and aortic chemoreceptors are chemosensitive cells stimulated by oxygen lack, CO2 excess, or H+ ion excess; located in the carotid bodies and aortic arch, they are not stimulated until arterial pressure falls below about 80 mm Hg, at which point their activation excites the vasomotor center. — Circulatory Physiology II.pptx, Slide 57"},{"q":"What triggers the CNS ischemic response, and how powerful is it compared to other pressure control mechanisms?","choices":["It is a weak, minor reflex triggered only by mild changes in cerebrospinal fluid pH, producing negligible and clinically insignificant effects on systemic arterial pressure","It is triggered by an excessively high cerebral blood flow that washes out CO2, which then inhibits the vasomotor centre and produces a modest, gradual decrease in the arterial pressure","It has no relationship to CO2 levels at all, and is instead triggered entirely by falling cerebral oxygen tension acting directly on the carotid body chemoreceptors","It is triggered by reduced cerebral blood flow causing CO2 buildup, which stimulates the vasomotor center to increase arterial pressure; it is one of the most powerful activators of the sympathetic vasoconstrictor system"],"answer":3,"exp":"The CNS ischemic response is activated by cerebral ischemia: reduced cerebral blood flow causes local CO2 buildup, which stimulates the vasomotor center and dramatically increases arterial pressure — this response is one of the most powerful activators of the sympathetic vasoconstrictor system, serving as an emergency mechanism to preserve brain perfusion. — Circulatory Physiology II.pptx, Slide 58"},{"q":"How do low-pressure receptors in the atria and pulmonary arteries respond to increased blood volume?","choices":["They minimize arterial pressure changes by lowering arterial pressure in response to increased blood volume, partly by enhancing Na/water excretion (decreased ADH, increased GFR, decreased Na reabsorption)","They only respond to arterial baroreceptor input relayed secondarily from the carotid sinus and aortic arch, with no independent sensitivity to atrial stretch","They amplify arterial pressure changes in response to increased blood volume, partly by suppressing Na and water excretion (increased ADH, decreased GFR)","They have no functional response to blood volume changes at all, since ADH secretion and the glomerular filtration rate are both controlled solely by the renin-angiotensin system"],"answer":0,"exp":"Low-pressure receptors in the atria and pulmonary arteries minimize arterial pressure changes in response to blood volume changes; increases in blood volume activate these receptors, which lower arterial pressure and enhance sodium/water excretion by decreasing ADH secretion, increasing glomerular filtration rate, and decreasing renal sodium reabsorption. — Circulatory Physiology II.pptx, Slide 59"},{"q":"How quickly can the vasomotor center (VMC), via the nervous system, increase arterial pressure?","choices":["Nervous control raises arterial pressure only gradually, over several hours, through slow catecholamine accumulation","Within seconds, by constricting arterioles, constricting large vessels, and directly increasing heart rate/contractility","Only over a period of several hours, by slowly increasing renal sodium retention and blood volume","Over several days, primarily through gradual remodeling of arteriolar smooth muscle"],"answer":1,"exp":"The nervous system, via the vasomotor center, can increase arterial pressure within seconds by constricting almost all arterioles (increasing TPR), constricting large vessels (increasing venous return/cardiac output), and directly increasing cardiac output via increased heart rate and contractility — explaining rapid pressure increases during exercise or fright. — Circulatory Physiology II.pptx, Slide 49"},{"q":"What is one everyday function the baroreceptor reflex serves related to posture?","choices":["It only functions during exercise and has no role in everyday postural blood pressure changes","It helps maintain relatively constant arterial pressure despite changes in body posture (e.g., standing up from lying down)","It has no relationship to posture changes, which are instead corrected entirely by the renin-angiotensin system","It causes a brief, exaggerated rise in blood pressure whenever a person changes from standing to lying down"],"answer":1,"exp":"The baroreceptor reflex helps maintain relatively constant arterial pressure despite changes in body posture (such as standing up), rapidly correcting the transient pressure drop that gravity would otherwise cause. — Circulatory Physiology II.pptx, Slide 55"},{"q":"How do baroreceptors affect daily variations in arterial pressure, despite adapting over the long term?","choices":["They actively amplify moment-to-moment pressure swings by positively reinforcing whatever direction pressure is already moving, substantially increasing daily blood pressure variability","They oppose either increases or decreases in arterial pressure moment-to-moment, thereby reducing daily variations in pressure, even though they adapt and are unimportant for true long-term (weeks-to-months) control","They only oppose increases in pressure and never decreases, since vagal center activation is physiologically incapable of falling below its resting baseline level","They have no effect on daily pressure variation at all, since baroreceptors are only capable of responding to sustained changes that last several weeks or months"],"answer":1,"exp":"Baroreceptors oppose either increases or decreases in arterial pressure as they occur throughout the day, reducing daily variations in pressure — even though, because they adapt/reset over sustained periods, they are considered unimportant for true long-term (weeks-to-months) blood pressure control. — Circulatory Physiology II.pptx, Slide 56"},{"q":"What type of nerve ending are baroreceptors, and in which specific arterial locations are they found?","choices":["Spray-type nerve endings found in the walls of the carotid sinus (at the carotid bifurcation) and in the walls of the aortic arch","Free nerve endings found only within the walls of the pulmonary artery, right atrium, and vena cava","Encapsulated Pacinian corpuscles found only within the walls of the ascending aorta and the coronary sinus","Chemoreceptor cells found exclusively within the carotid and aortic bodies, not the sinus wall"],"answer":0,"exp":"Baroreceptors are spray-type nerve endings located in the walls of the carotid sinus (at the carotid bifurcation) and in the walls of the aortic arch, distinct from the nearby chemoreceptor cells of the carotid and aortic bodies. — Circulatory Physiology II.pptx, Slide 51"},{"q":"During which phase of the cardiac cycle is coronary perfusion of the heart muscle greatest?","choices":["Diastole, when the myocardium is relaxed and no longer compressing the coronary vessels","Systole, when the open aortic valve allows maximal pressure into the coronary ostia","Only during isovolumic relaxation, briefly, between diastole and systole","Coronary perfusion is greatest during systole, when intraventricular pressure peaks"],"answer":0,"exp":"Coronary perfusion is greatest during diastole, when the relaxed myocardium no longer compresses the coronary vessels running through it; during systole, muscle contraction obstructs coronary flow. — Circulatory Physiology II.pptx, Slide 40"},{"q":"In the coronary circulation, do local metabolic factors or neural (sympathetic) influences dominate the regulation of blood flow?","choices":["Only neural sympathetic influences regulate coronary flow; local metabolic byproducts play no meaningful role","Metabolic factors override neural influences on coronary flow, just as they do in skeletal muscle","Neither metabolic nor neural factors influence coronary flow, which instead is set entirely by vessel diameter","Neural influences always dominate coronary flow, unlike the situation seen in skeletal muscle"],"answer":1,"exp":"In the coronary circulation, just as in skeletal muscle, metabolic factors override neural influences in determining blood flow — even though sympathetic stimulation can increase cardiac activity (and thus metabolite production) and directly affect vessel tone via adrenergic receptors. — Circulatory Physiology II.pptx, Slide 41"},{"q":"How does an intact renin-angiotensin system affect the drop in arterial pressure during severe hemorrhage, compared to when the system is blocked?","choices":["The RAS has no measurable effect on hemorrhage-induced pressure drops, since baroreceptor reflexes alone fully restore pressure to 50 mm Hg","An intact RAS actually accelerates the initial pressure drop by triggering a rapid renal sodium and water loss during the acute phase of a severe hemorrhage","An intact RAS helps buffer the drop in arterial pressure; if the RAS is blocked, arterial pressure does not recover as well after a pressure drop (e.g., to 50 mm Hg from severe hemorrhage)","Blocking the RAS has essentially no effect on pressure recovery, since aldosterone release from the adrenal cortex compensates fully and independently"],"answer":2,"exp":"Severe hemorrhage can lower arterial pressure to as low as 50 mm Hg; when the renin-angiotensin system is intact, it helps buffer this drop and supports pressure recovery, but if the RAS is blocked, arterial pressure does not increase much further, showing the RAS's protective role during acute hypotension. — Circulatory Physiology II.pptx, Slide 11"},{"q":"How does increased sodium intake influence extracellular fluid volume (ECFV)?","choices":["Increased Na intake stimulates drinking and ADH secretion (via increased Na concentration/thirst), and changes in Na intake lead to changes in ECFV, since ECFV is determined by the balance of Na intake and output","Sodium intake has no relationship whatsoever to ECFV, since the extracellular fluid volume is regulated exclusively by aldosterone-independent renal water handling mechanisms","ECFV is determined solely by dietary potassium intake and its renal excretion, entirely independent of sodium balance or plasma osmolarity fluctuations at all","Increased sodium intake always decreases ECFV by triggering an osmotic diuresis that substantially exceeds any accompanying water retention response in the kidney"],"answer":0,"exp":"As sodium intake increases, it stimulates drinking and (via increased plasma Na concentration) stimulates thirst and ADH secretion; because ECFV is determined by the balance of Na intake and output, changes in Na intake directly lead to changes in extracellular fluid volume. — Circulatory Physiology II.pptx, Slide 7"},{"q":"Do different arterial pressure control mechanisms (nervous, RAAS, renal-fluid volume) act over the same timescale?","choices":["Yes — all mechanisms (nervous, hormonal, and renal) reach maximal effect within seconds and sustain identical potency indefinitely thereafter","Only the hormonal RAAS system has any meaningful timing characteristics; nervous and renal mechanisms act with equal, instantaneous potency","No — different mechanisms operate with varying potency over different time intervals, from rapid nervous reflexes (seconds) to the renal-body fluid mechanism, which dominates long-term control","The renal-body fluid mechanism actually acts the fastest of all, within seconds, while the nervous baroreceptor reflexes take days to weeks to become fully effective"],"answer":2,"exp":"Different arterial pressure control mechanisms operate with varying potency across different time intervals — nervous reflexes (like the baroreceptor reflex) act within seconds, hormonal mechanisms (like RAAS) act over minutes to hours, and the renal-body fluid mechanism dominates true long-term (days to weeks) pressure control. — Circulatory Physiology II.pptx, Slide 17"},{"q":"Why does skeletal muscle blood flow show a rhythmical pattern during rhythmic contraction (e.g., walking)?","choices":["Flow only increases during relaxation and remains fixed at a constant level throughout every contraction phase","Rhythmical flow patterns occur only in smooth muscle organs like the gut, never in skeletal muscle","Vessels are compressed during contraction (reducing flow), so flow rhythmically varies with the contraction-relaxation cycle","Blood flow rises continuously throughout exercise with no rhythmical variation tied to the contraction cycle"],"answer":2,"exp":"Rhythmical contraction of skeletal muscle causes rhythmical blood flow because the vessels running through the muscle are compressed during contraction (reducing flow) and reopen during relaxation (restoring flow). — Circulatory Physiology II.pptx, Slide 36"},{"q":"What happens to previously inactive (\"dormant\") skeletal muscle capillaries during exercise?","choices":["They constrict further during exercise, reducing flow to the resting fibers nearby","They open only during rest and close completely once exercise begins","They open during exercise, increasing nutrient delivery to the active tissue","Dormant capillaries exist only within resting cardiac muscle tissue"],"answer":2,"exp":"At rest, some skeletal muscle capillaries have little or no blood flow (\"dormant\"); during exercise, these dormant capillaries open, increasing the capillary surface area available for nutrient delivery to the active muscle. — Circulatory Physiology II.pptx, Slide 36"},{"q":"What is notable about sympathetic vasodilator nerves in skeletal muscle blood flow control?","choices":["They have no role in skeletal muscle circulation and are found exclusively in the cutaneous vasculature of the limbs","They are activated by feed-forward stimulation in some animal species, providing anticipatory vasodilation before exercise even begins","They only cause vasoconstriction via alpha receptors, with no anticipatory or feed-forward function in any species","They are the dominant mechanism of exercise hyperemia in humans, overriding local metabolic vasodilation entirely"],"answer":1,"exp":"Sympathetic vasodilator nerves, activated by \"feed-forward\" stimulation in some animal species, can anticipate the onset of exercise and begin dilating skeletal muscle vessels even before local metabolic factors from muscle activity take over as the dominant control mechanism. — Circulatory Physiology II.pptx, Slide 37"}]};
